          "field": "etymology",
          "verdict": "issue",
          "quote": "Asleep was formed from an old short form of on or in plus sleep",
          "severity": "blocking",
          "family": "etymology",
          "reason": "The origin is inaccurately and uncertainly stated: asleep developed from Old English on slæpe, meaning “in sleep”; it was not formed from an alternative “on or in” element."
        },
        {
          "field": "etymology",
          "verdict": "issue",
          "quote": "That is why it acts like a phrase after a verb and never comes before a noun",
          "severity": "blocking",
          "family": "etymology",
          "reason": "The etymology does not establish that modern syntactic restriction, and asleep is not limited to occurring after a verb because it also occurs in constructions such as asleep in bed."
        },
        {
          "field": "see_also",
          "verdict": "ok",
          "quote": null,
          "severity": null,
          "family": null,
          "reason": null
        },
        {
          "field": "adaptation",
          "verdict": "issue",
          "quote": "Asleep is used only after a verb such as be, fall, or lie",
          "severity": "blocking",
          "family": "adaptation",
          "reason": "This is too absolute: asleep can occur without immediately following a verb, as in “the baby asleep in bed” or “the baby is asleep in bed.”"
        },
        {
          "field": "adaptation",
          "verdict": "issue",
          "quote": "the same is true of awake, alive, alone, and afraid",
          "severity": "blocking",
          "family": "adaptation",
          "reason": "The claim is false for afraid, which can occur before a noun in established uses such as “an afraid child,” though this is less common than predicative use."
        },
